WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.120
While the Trump administration's tariffs
on steel, aluminum, and Chinese-made goods

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.620
have earned most of the headlines, another more obscure set of Trump backed import duties

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.620
are hitting Americans squarely in the booze cabinet.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.720
Since October, the US has charged a new 25% tariff on single malt scotch whiskey,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.860
part of a broader set of levies targeting hundreds of European cultural items,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.460
including Italian pasta, German ham, and English wool.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:31.360
The 25% tariff is expected to cause Scotch exports to the United States to drop by 20% over a year.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.460
Practically, the tariff means that what used to be a $40 bottle of scotch is now going to set you back

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.700
more than 50 bucks. Smaller distilleries will be particularly hard hit

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.600
because the tariffs specifically targets single malt scotch whiskey,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.880
that means that blended varieties like those sold by most major brands are exempt.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.200
So those prices will likely remain the same. Some small batch producers, like the Fife based

00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:00.120
Kings Barn Distillery, are reportedly delaying their plans to tap into the lucrative and growing US market

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.560
because of the new tariffs. Trump has repeatedly reached for tariffs, which are really nothing more than

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:11.400
taxes paid by American importers and consumers, as part of a misguided attempt to force other countries

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.720
to negotiate trade deals with the US. But the Scotch tariffs are somewhat separate

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.180
from the current administration's myriad trade wars. Whiskey has become collateral damage

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.800
in a long-running spat between the US and the European Union over subsidies to,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.080
of all things, airplane manufacturers.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.800
The World Trade Organization gave the US permission to impose those tariffs on EU exports last year,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.560
including on whiskey, after the US claimed that Airbus was being unfairly subsidized by the French government.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.840
The main rival to Airbus is of course Washington state based Boeing, a heavy hitter in American politics

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.780
that's also the recipient of generous corporate welfare.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.800
The whole episode demonstrates the foolishness of tariffs. American consumers of whiskey, wine, and wool

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.460
are not subsidizing Airbus, but people and
businesses on both sides of the Atlantic

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.500
are being punished simply for trying to amicably trade what they have for what they want.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.980
Whiskey distillers have always had to account for the angels share,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:11.840
that small amounts of whiskey that are lost to evaporation during the aging process,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.080
but now thanks to the Trump administration, they have to plan for the tariff man's cut too.

